            {
              "id": "glog-1d57ed07-935b-4090-b14b-3563b9b7e054",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source is larger than the destination. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming, and can lead to serious security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `memcpy(sd->mreqs, nla_data(mc), nla_len(mc))`, the `memcpy` function is used to copy data from `nla_data(mc)` to `sd->mreqs` with a length of `nla_len(mc)`. If `nla_len(mc)` is larger than the size of `sd->mreqs`, it can cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source data. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for the provided code snippet could be:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = nla_len(mc);\nif (len <= sizeof(sd->mreqs)) {\n    memcpy(sd->mreqs, nla_data(mc), len);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the size of `sd->mreqs` is large enough to hold `nla_data(mc)`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If not, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to be using the `memcpy` function from the C Standard Library (`<cstring>` in C++), and `nla_data` and `nla_len` functions from the Netlink Library (`<linux/netlink.h>` in Linux).\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
